["fast","faster"]},{"number":3,"question":"We are going ___ book tomorrow.","options":["to","for"]},{"number":4,"question":"There are not ___ chairs.","options":["enough","much"]},{"number":5,"question":"You must ___ closed shoes.","options":["wear","wearing"]},{"number":6,"question":"I have never ___ pottery.","options":["try","tried"]},{"number":7,"question":"She was ___ when he called.","options":["packing","packed"]},{"number":8,"question":"He enjoys ___ new skills.","options":["learning","learn"]},{"number":9,"question":"If it rains, we ___ stay inside.","options":["will","would"]},{"number":10,"question":"You do not ___ to bring tools.","options":["have","must"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"Nour planned a weekend pottery course in a coastal town. She booked a room near the station because it was cheaper than the rooms beside the beach. On Friday evening, she was packing when the organiser called. The first session had moved from Saturday morning to Saturday afternoon. Nour kept her train ticket and decided to visit a market before the course. Participants must wear closed shoes and bring an old shirt. They do not have to bring tools because the studio provides them. Nour has tried painting before, but she has never made a bowl. If she enjoys the weekend, she will join the monthly course. She wants to practise regularly rather than buy lots of expensive equipment immediately.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Why did Nour choose the room near the station?"},{"number":2,"question":"What was she doing when the organiser called?"},{"number":3,"question":"When will the first session now take place?"},{"number":4,"question":"What must participants wear and bring?"},{"number":5,"question":"What will Nour do if she enjoys the weekend?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"How many people have now registered?"},{"number":2,"question":"Which food is needed?"},{"number":3,"question":"By what time on Sunday should it arrive?"},{"number":4,"question":"Who is supplying the drinks?"},{"number":5,"question":"By when should volunteers tell Karim about limited availability?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write to a friend about a planned weekend course. Explain your choice, compare two options, describe a change to the plan and say what you will do if the course goes well.","wordMin":80,"wordMax":100,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"3":{"version":3,"level":3,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 3","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_3_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["storage","confirm","alternative","interview","settle in","appointment","strategy","commute","inspection","sort"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"become comfortable in a new place"},{"number":2,"question":"another possible choice"},{"number":3,"question":"a careful check"},{"number":4,"question":"a planned way of achieving something"},{"number":5,"question":"space for keeping things"},{"number":6,"question":"separate items into groups"},{"number":7,"question":"a planned conversation for collecting information"},{"number":8,"question":"travel regularly between home and work"},{"number":9,"question":"state that an arrangement is certain"},{"number":10,"question":"an arranged time to meet or receive a service"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"I have lived here ___ May.","options":["for","since"]},{"number":2,"question":"If the form fails, staff ___ help.","options":["will","would"]},{"number":3,"question":"Could you tell me where the office ___?","options":["is","does"]},{"number":4,"question":"We have already ___ the request.","options":["send","sent"]},{"number":5,"question":"Residents ___ need help can call.","options":["who","which"]},{"number":6,"question":"The machine was ___ on Monday.","options":["repair","repaired"]},{"number":7,"question":"She said that she ___ busy.","options":["was","has"]},{"number":8,"question":"They have been ___ for an hour.","options":["wait","waiting"]},{"number":9,"question":"Before the meeting, he had ___ the list.","options":["check","checked"]},{"number":10,"question":"If I had more time, I ___ volunteer.","options":["will","would"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"After moving to West Lane, Leila found that the shared laundry room was often crowded. She has lived in the building for six months and has been recording waiting times for the last two weeks. The manager suggested a booking system, but some residents cannot use the online form. Leila proposed keeping a paper list as well. Before the meeting, the caretaker had checked the machines and found that one had been out of use for three days. It was repaired on Monday. Residents who use the room after work still report long waits. The manager said that the paper list would be placed beside the door. Leila would prefer a longer trial before making the system permanent. She has asked whether the results can be reviewed after four weeks, using both waiting times and comments from residents.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"How long has Leila lived in the building?"},{"number":2,"question":"Why was a paper list proposed?"},{"number":3,"question":"What had the caretaker discovered?"},{"number":4,"question":"Where will the paper list be placed?"},{"number":5,"question":"What two kinds of evidence should the review use?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"When did testing begin?"},{"number":2,"question":"What has been rewritten?"},{"number":3,"question":"Does a request confirm an appointment?"},{"number":4,"question":"What should people with a reference number avoid?"},{"number":5,"question":"What feedback is specifically requested?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write a request to the manager of a shared building. Describe a problem that has continued for some time, explain what has already been tried, propose a practical improvement and ask politely when it can be reviewed.","wordMin":110,"wordMax":140,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"4":{"version":3,"level":4,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 4","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_4_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["requirement","assumption","evidence","dependency","transition","accessible","routine","qualification","proposal","sequence"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"a change from one situation to another"},{"number":2,"question":"information used to support a claim"},{"number":3,"question":"a suggested plan"},{"number":4,"question":"a regular way of doing things"},{"number":5,"question":"something that is needed or expected"},{"number":6,"question":"the order in which things happen"},{"number":7,"question":"something that relies on another condition"},{"number":8,"question":"a recognised record of study or skill"},{"number":9,"question":"something accepted without checking"},{"number":10,"question":"easy for people to reach or use"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"If access were easier, more people ___ attend.","options":["would","will"]},{"number":2,"question":"She ___ be at work; I am not certain. (possibility)","options":["might","must"]},{"number":3,"question":"___ the room is larger, access is harder.","options":["Although","Despite"]},{"number":4,"question":"I am used to ___ early.","options":["arrive","arriving"]},{"number":5,"question":"The manager, ___ joined years ago, agreed.","options":["who","which"]},{"number":6,"question":"He said that the meeting ___ postponed.","options":["was","has"]},{"number":7,"question":"By Friday, we will have ___ the trial.","options":["finish","finished"]},{"number":8,"question":"If she had known, she would have ___.","options":["help","helped"]},{"number":9,"question":"We should have ___ the entrance.","options":["check","checked"]},{"number":10,"question":"We had the system ___.","options":["check","checked"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"The community centre is considering moving its evening classes to a larger building. The new site has better equipment but is farther from the bus stop. A planning note says that the move must be popular because online comments are mostly positive. A volunteer challenges that inference: the comments come from people who chose to reply, and attendance records have not been examined. She acknowledges that the equipment would be useful, while arguing that access deserves a separate check. The manager, who has worked at the centre for nine years, proposes a four-week trial. By the end of the trial, the team will have compared attendance at both sites. One tutor says the old timetable should have been reviewed earlier. However, the group does not assume that an earlier review would necessarily have prevented the current difficulty. The final decision remains open pending the trial and transport information.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What benefit and drawback does the new site have?"},{"number":2,"question":"Why are positive comments insufficient to establish popularity?"},{"number":3,"question":"What does the volunteer concede?"},{"number":4,"question":"What will the team have compared by the end of the trial?"},{"number":5,"question":"Which two sources of information will inform the final decision?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What did the tutor actually recommend?"},{"number":2,"question":"What will be checked before the trial?"},{"number":3,"question":"What drawback does the new room have?"},{"number":4,"question":"Does earlier transport information guarantee a different decision?"},{"number":5,"question":"When should comments reach the organiser?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write a recommendation about moving the classes described in the reading. Compare the benefits and limits, acknowledge one valid opposing point, distinguish evidence from inference and propose a reviewable next step.","wordMin":150,"wordMax":180,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"5":{"version":3,"level":5,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 5","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_5_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["inclusive","warranty","maintenance","rationale","forecast","recollection","respondent","likelihood","viewpoint","emphasis"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"an estimate of a future result"},{"number":2,"question":"work that keeps something in good condition"},{"number":3,"question":"the position from which a story is told"},{"number":4,"question":"a person who answers a survey"},{"number":5,"question":"designed to involve different people"},{"number":6,"question":"special attention given to something"},{"number":7,"question":"the reason supporting a decision"},{"number":8,"question":"the chance that something will happen"},{"number":9,"question":"a promise about repair or replacement under stated terms"},{"number":10,"question":"a remembered account of something"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"If the hours had not changed, the comparison ___ be clearer now.","options":["would","will"]},{"number":2,"question":"The system appears ___ help some users.","options":["to","for"]},{"number":3,"question":"Earlier, she had ___ away.","options":["move","moved"]},{"number":4,"question":"Each respondent ___ one form. (past)","options":["completed","complete"]},{"number":5,"question":"Having ___ the survey, she revised the claim.","options":["read","reading"]},{"number":6,"question":"What matters ___ the missing group.","options":["is","are"]},{"number":7,"question":"The team requested an ___ of the issue.","options":["explain","explanation"]},{"number":8,"question":"They should have ___ unsuccessful users.","options":["include","included"]},{"number":9,"question":"The finding is limited ___ the respondents.","options":["to","at"]},{"number":10,"question":"Four people had difficulty. These ___ matter.","options":["responses","response"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"A local reading room reports that its new booking system has improved access. The evidence is a survey of sixteen volunteers, twelve of whom say that booking is easier. The remaining four report difficulty finding a suitable time. The survey does not include people who stopped trying to book. A reviewer accepts that the responses describe these volunteers\u2019 experience, but questions the headline \u201cAccess improved for everyone.\u201d She also notes that longer opening hours began in the same month. The records do not separate the effects of the system from the effect of those hours. A revised report names the respondents, describes the four difficulties and avoids claiming a single cause. Having examined the survey, the reviewer recommends collecting information from unsuccessful applicants. What the report needs is a clearer account of who is missing, rather than a more confident adjective. The team agrees to retain the positive responses while narrowing the conclusion.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"How many volunteers reported easier booking?"},{"number":2,"question":"Which group is missing from the survey?"},{"number":3,"question":"Which simultaneous change complicates a causal claim?"},{"number":4,"question":"What does the reviewer accept?"},{"number":5,"question":"What additional information is recommended?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"When had the writer used the reading room regularly?"},{"number":2,"question":"Which overbroad statement is revised?"},{"number":3,"question":"Who helped her?"},{"number":4,"question":"Why is the feeling of exclusion retained?"},{"number":5,"question":"What practical change is suggested?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write an evaluation of the reading-room report. State what the survey supports, explain two limits, discuss an alternative explanation and make one justified recommendation. Keep the scope of every claim clear.","wordMin":180,"wordMax":210,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"6":{"version":3,"level":6,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 6","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_6_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["consent","method","causation","polarisation","premise","tradition","principle","inference","identity","attribution"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"a starting statement supporting an argument"},{"number":2,"question":"a relationship in which one factor produces another"},{"number":3,"question":"a sense of who a person or group is"},{"number":4,"question":"a general belief guiding decisions"},{"number":5,"question":"agreement given with relevant understanding"},{"number":6,"question":"identification of whose idea is being presented"},{"number":7,"question":"movement towards strongly opposed positions"},{"number":8,"question":"a conclusion drawn from information"},{"number":9,"question":"the procedure used to investigate"},{"number":10,"question":"a practice passed between generations"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Only after checking ___ she notice the problem. (past simple inversion)","options":["did","had"]},{"number":2,"question":"The label may ___ reduced confusion.","options":["have","has"]},{"number":3,"question":"Having ___ the interface, she wrote a report.","options":["examine","examined"]},{"number":4,"question":"They recommend that the setting ___ visible.","options":["be","being"]},{"number":5,"question":"The evidence applies ___ this trial.","options":["to","at"]},{"number":6,"question":"The reviewer is said ___ support clearer labels.","options":["to","for"]},{"number":7,"question":"Even ___ a default helps, alternatives still matter. (hypothetical)","options":["if","despite"]},{"number":8,"question":"This ___ needs explanation.","options":["finding","they"]},{"number":9,"question":"The report requests greater ___.","options":["visible","visibility"]},{"number":10,"question":"She can change it, and so ___ I.","options":["can","do"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"A neighbourhood archive introduces an online catalogue and describes it as giving visitors complete control over their searches. The default display ranks results by an unexplained rule, and changing that order requires opening a separate menu. A reviewer argues that a choice can exist without being easy to notice. Only after examining the interface did she question the phrase complete control. She does not claim that all defaults are harmful: a starting arrangement can help new users. Her recommendation is that the active setting be visible and that the alternative be explained beside it. A small trial records fewer requests for help after a label is added, but the trial also coincides with extra staff support. The conclusion therefore distinguishes observed change from its uncertain cause. The archive\u2019s summary attributes the recommendation to the reviewer rather than presenting it as the unanimous view of visitors. A further comparison is proposed before any broader claim about independent searching.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Why might an available choice still be difficult to use?"},{"number":2,"question":"What benefit of defaults does the reviewer acknowledge?"},{"number":3,"question":"What two changes does she recommend?"},{"number":4,"question":"What complicates the causal interpretation of fewer help requests?"},{"number":5,"question":"Why is attribution important in the summary?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What did the earlier version claim?"},{"number":2,"question":"What distinction does the revision make?"},{"number":3,"question":"Was the alternative still available?"},{"number":4,"question":"Which preference does the group acknowledge?"},{"number":5,"question":"What must remain clear in a non-endorsing summary?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write a critical summary of the archive case. Attribute the recommendation, acknowledge a benefit of defaults, evaluate the trial\u2019s limit and propose wording that preserves both choice and uncertainty.","wordMin":200,"wordMax":230,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"7":{"version":3,"level":7,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 7","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_7_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["focalisation","representation","stereotype","reversible","epistemic","rhetoric","hypothesis","provisional","agent","procedure"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"connected with knowledge and certainty"},{"number":2,"question":"an oversimplified belief about a group"},{"number":3,"question":"the person or thing performing an action"},{"number":4,"question":"a proposed explanation to be tested"},{"number":5,"question":"the perspective through which events are perceived"},{"number":6,"question":"an established sequence of actions"},{"number":7,"question":"able to be undone"},{"number":8,"question":"subject to further change or checking"},{"number":9,"question":"the way people or ideas are portrayed"},{"number":10,"question":"language chosen to influence an audience"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"The interviews are said ___ describe experience.","options":["to","for"]},{"number":2,"question":"The review involves checking, comparing and ___.","options":["recording","records"]},{"number":3,"question":"Publication requires clear ___.","options":["accountable","accountability"]},{"number":4,"question":"More visits ___ indicate interest, but do not prove accuracy.","options":["may","definitely"]},{"number":5,"question":"The entries ___ yesterday need review.","options":["added","adding"]},{"number":6,"question":"It is essential that errors ___ recorded.","options":["be","being"]},{"number":7,"question":"By Monday, staff will have ___ the section.","options":["check","checked"]},{"number":8,"question":"The claim must be consistent ___ the evidence.","options":["with","to"]},{"number":9,"question":"Only after approval ___ expansion begin.","options":["will","it will"]},{"number":10,"question":"The conclusion is limited ___ this section.","options":["to","at"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"Three accounts of a local archive project reach different conclusions. Attendance records show more visits after a digital catalogue was introduced. Interviews describe easier searching for some visitors, while a review of twelve record entries finds two unresolved errors. A draft report calls the catalogue an unqualified success and proposes discarding the paper index. The editor rejects that conclusion. Increased use is not equivalent to reliable retrieval, and the sources answer different questions. The archive coordinator proposes digitising one further section while retaining the originals. Expansion will stop if the review finds unresolved mismatches in that section. The review team must define a mismatch before beginning so that the threshold is not adjusted to fit the result. By the next meeting, the team will have checked the new entries. The report will then distinguish the reversible trial from the later, harder-to-reverse disposal decision. Caution about disposal is compatible with a clear next action.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What does the attendance source show?"},{"number":2,"question":"How many reviewed entries have unresolved errors?"},{"number":3,"question":"Why is unqualified success too strong?"},{"number":4,"question":"What must be defined before review begins?"},{"number":5,"question":"Why are trial expansion and disposal separated?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Who will publish the review?"},{"number":2,"question":"When will it be published?"},{"number":3,"question":"Who decides on wider expansion?"},{"number":4,"question":"Why was improvements will be implemented removed?"},{"number":5,"question":"Why are some details withheld?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write a recommendation to the archive committee. Synthesize the three sources, explain the risk of the proposed conclusion, distinguish reversible from irreversible action and set out a justified next step.","wordMin":200,"wordMax":230,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"8":{"version":3,"level":8,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 8","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Speak_Boldly_GE_8_Exam.pdf","instructions":"Work independently without a dictionary or coursebooks. Read each instruction carefully. This is a course achievement exam; it does not certify a CEFR level. Speaking is assessed separately by your instructor. All cases are fictional.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Vocabulary in meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose a word from the word bank for each definition. Use each word once. Each item is worth 2 marks.","wordBank":["inference","commensurable","divergence","portfolio","precision","credibility","steelman","redundancy","qualification","granularity"],"questions":[{"number":1,"question":"exactness in wording and meaning"},{"number":2,"question":"movement towards difference"},{"number":3,"question":"a limit placed on a claim"},{"number":4,"question":"a strong and fair reconstruction of an opposing argument"},{"number":5,"question":"a conclusion drawn from observed details"},{"number":6,"question":"the level of detail used"},{"number":7,"question":"a selected collection showing work and development"},{"number":8,"question":"unnecessary repetition"},{"number":9,"question":"capable of meaningful comparison"},{"number":10,"question":"the quality of being believable for good reasons"}]},"B":{"title":"B. Grammar and intended meaning","marks":20,"instruction":"Choose the correct option. Where a meaning is specified, choose the form that expresses that meaning. Each item is worth 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"The condition depends ___ approval.","options":["on","of"]},{"number":2,"question":"The interviews are reported ___ describe ease of use.","options":["to","for"]},{"number":3,"question":"Earlier, staff had ___ a guarantee.","options":["publish","published"]},{"number":4,"question":"Even ___ the form helps, it cannot guarantee timing. (hypothetical)","options":["if","despite"]},{"number":5,"question":"The editor revised the first sentence and the second ___.","options":["one","ones"]},{"number":6,"question":"Requests ___ during the trial were reviewed.","options":["received","receiving"]},{"number":7,"question":"The task involves selecting, editing and ___.","options":["reflecting","reflection"]},{"number":8,"question":"Staff checked ___ the trial requests, no others.","options":["only","all"]},{"number":9,"question":"They should have ___ the scope.","options":["check","checked"]},{"number":10,"question":"What matters ___ the evidence.","options":["is","are"]}]},"C":{"title":"C. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the passage and answer using evidence from the text. Each question is worth 4 marks.","passage":"A regional service office publishes a guide saying that its new request form guarantees a reply within two days. The trial behind that statement involved ten requests: eight received a reply within two days and two took longer. A separate staff note records fewer clarification emails, while interviews with four applicants describe the form as easier to follow. The editor separates those outcomes rather than treating them as three proofs of the same claim. The office agrees to retract the guarantee. A proposed replacement says that replies may take longer, but the editor asks for the observed result as well, so that caution does not become vague. The revised guide states the trial figures and identifies the next action for applicants whose requests remain unanswered. A short public version and a longer explanatory note retain the same factual core. The longer note explains the trial\u2019s limitations; neither version claims that the form alone caused faster replies. The editorial rationale records why the universal claim changed and why the distinction between ease of use and response time remained.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What trial result contradicts the guarantee?"},{"number":2,"question":"Which two other outcomes appear in the sources?"},{"number":3,"question":"Why does may take longer alone not satisfy the editor?"},{"number":4,"question":"What must the short and long versions share?"},{"number":5,"question":"Which causal claim is avoided?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions for one minute. Your coach will read the passage live twice. Write short answers. Each question is worth 4 marks.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Which three text types must be included?"},{"number":2,"question":"What must accompany each final text?"},{"number":3,"question":"What three elements should commentary contain?"},{"number":4,"question":"What should at least one example retain?"},{"number":5,"question":"What does the transfer task require?"}]},"E":{"title":"E. Writing","marks":20,"instruction":"Write an editorial rationale for revising the service-office guide. Include an accurate replacement for the guarantee, distinguish the three source outcomes and explain how the short version can remain accessible without changing certainty or scope.","wordMin":200,"wordMax":230,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task completion and relevant detail","marks":5},{"criterion":"Organisation and connection","marks":5},{"criterion":"Vocabulary appropriate to purpose","marks":5},{"criterion":"Grammar and written accuracy","marks":5}]}}},"9":{"version":4,"level":9,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 9","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Level_9_Candidate_Paper.pdf","instructions":"Answer every question. Work independently without a dictionary, phone or outside assistance. Use your own words for reading answers where possible; short, accurate answers are acceptable. All situations and data are fictional assessment material. Your coach reads the listening text live twice; pause other work during the readings. Speaking is assessed separately and does not change the core total out of 100. The website time limit is 45 minutes.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Language in context and production","marks":30,"instruction":"Questions 1\u201310: choose one answer. Questions 11\u201315: write one sentence for each task. Each response earns up to 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"If the tutor confirms, we ___ the room.","options":["will book","would booked","had booked","booking"]},{"number":2,"question":"The room, ___ holds twenty people, is available on Thursdays.","options":["who","where","which","whose"]},{"number":3,"question":"We need to ___ a decision before Friday.","options":["do","make","have done","give"]},{"number":4,"question":"She asked whether I ___ attend the meeting the following day.","options":["can","will","could","may"]},{"number":5,"question":"The materials must ___ before the course begins.","options":["order","be ordered","ordering","have order"]},{"number":6,"question":"___ the low price, the course attracted only six students.","options":["Although","Because","Despite","However"]},{"number":7,"question":"I wish we ___ more time to advertise.","options":["have","had","will have","having"]},{"number":8,"question":"They have been discussing the proposal ___ Monday.","options":["for","during","since","until"]},{"number":9,"question":"The centre is responsible ___ checking the equipment.","options":["for","to","with","of"]},{"number":10,"question":"Which sentence makes a cautious recommendation?","options":["This will certainly work.","There is no possible risk.","You must agree with me.","It might be worth running a trial."]},{"number":11,"question":"Politely ask the library to confirm its staffing arrangements by Friday."},{"number":12,"question":"Combine with although: The room is cheap. It is too small for all twenty learners."},{"number":13,"question":"Report this statement: Sara said, \"I can help next week.\""},{"number":14,"question":"Explain one condition for booking the library room, using provided that."},{"number":15,"question":"Correct this sentence: We look forward to hear from you."}]},"B":{"title":"B. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read all three sources, then answer using evidence from the sources. Each answer earns up to 4 marks.","passage":"Source A The coordinator Twenty residents have expressed interest in a digital skills course. The community centre offers twelve seats on Tuesday at no charge. The library can seat twenty on Thursday, but its manager cannot confirm staffing until Friday. Both venues have the computers required. We should not assume that a room offer includes a tutor.\n\nSource B The survey Sixteen of the twenty interested residents answered a short survey. Twelve said they could attend on Thursday; four could not. The remaining four residents have not responded. The survey did not ask about Tuesday availability. A volunteer has offered to help learners practise, but has not agreed to lead the course or committed to every session.\n\nSource C The committee The total budget is 300 units. The library room would cost 240 and printed materials would cost 40. No payment is needed for the centre room. The committee may recommend a venue, but only the director can sign a booking. It cannot promise extra funding. The committee must send its recommendation on Monday. A smaller pilot is possible, provided the selection process is fair and applicants understand that further places are not guaranteed.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Compare the capacity and room cost of the two venues."},{"number":2,"question":"Explain two reasons why the survey cannot show which venue all twenty residents prefer."},{"number":3,"question":"Calculate the library option total and remaining budget. Explain one staffing uncertainty."},{"number":4,"question":"State what the volunteer offered, what commitment is missing, and two limits on the committee."},{"number":5,"question":"Recommend a next step and justify it with three relevant details from the sources."}]},"C":{"title":"C. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions before the first reading. Your coach reads the passage live twice. You may take notes; pause other work during the readings. Each question earns 4 marks: two details worth 2 marks each.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"How many items were returned on time, and how many borrowers returned items late?"},{"number":2,"question":"What happened to the Wednesday collection proposal, and why?"},{"number":3,"question":"Who will draft the reminder, and by when?"},{"number":4,"question":"Who will review the results, and when?"},{"number":5,"question":"What happens to the deposit, and what action was approved today?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Writing","marks":30,"instruction":"Write a 230-260 word email to the director recommending a venue or a conditional pilot for the digital skills course. Use Sources A-C. Compare the options, explain two uncertainties, and request a clear next action. Do not treat unconfirmed arrangements as facts. After the email, add a separate 30-40 word note explaining one language choice you made for a polite professional tone.","wordMin":230,"wordMax":260,"commentaryMin":30,"commentaryMax":40,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task and evidence","marks":10},{"criterion":"Organisation","marks":5},{"criterion":"Language range","marks":5},{"criterion":"Accuracy","marks":5},{"criterion":"Audience and commentary","marks":5}]}}},"10":{"version":4,"level":10,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 10","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Level_10_Candidate_Paper.pdf","instructions":"Answer every question. Work independently without a dictionary, phone or outside assistance. Use your own words for reading answers where possible; short, accurate answers are acceptable. All situations and data are fictional assessment material. Your coach reads the listening text live twice; pause other work during the readings. Speaking is assessed separately and does not change the core total out of 100. The website time limit is 45 minutes.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Language in context and production","marks":30,"instruction":"Questions 1\u201310: choose one answer. Questions 11\u201315: write one sentence for each task. Each response earns up to 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Had the council consulted residents, it ___ the access problem earlier.","options":["would identify","would have identified","will identify","has identified"]},{"number":2,"question":"Not only ___ the figures, but she also corrected the headline.","options":["she checked","checked she","did she check","she did check"]},{"number":3,"question":"The poll is informative; ___, it does not represent every resident.","options":["therefore","however","because","unless"]},{"number":4,"question":"The trial is said ___ public interest last month.","options":["to attract","attracting","to have attracted","attracted"]},{"number":5,"question":"Which is a concession?","options":["The cost is irrelevant.","Only one view matters.","Admittedly, access remains a concern.","There is no disagreement."]},{"number":6,"question":"We objected to the claim ___ the poll proved universal support.","options":["what","that","who","whose"]},{"number":7,"question":"The phrase \"a perfectly relaxing trip\" describes a journey full of breakdowns. Its likely purpose is ___.","options":["literal praise","irony","a legal definition","a timetable"]},{"number":8,"question":"The committee insisted that the route ___ accessible.","options":["be","being","was being","to be"]},{"number":9,"question":"The increase coincided ___ a music event.","options":["at","for","with","on"]},{"number":10,"question":"Which claim is best supported by 120 positive responses out of 150 online respondents?","options":["All residents support the plan.","Eighty percent of respondents supported it.","Most residents voted.","No one opposed it."]},{"number":11,"question":"Rewrite with inversion: We had rarely seen such strong opposition."},{"number":12,"question":"Make this claim appropriately cautious: The trial caused the increase in visits."},{"number":13,"question":"Acknowledge a benefit, then raise an access concern in one sentence."},{"number":14,"question":"Report: The chair said, \"We approved the trial yesterday.\""},{"number":15,"question":"Correct: Despite the route was popular, it remained temporary."}]},"B":{"title":"B. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read all three sources, then answer using evidence from the sources. Each answer earns up to 4 marks.","passage":"Source A Campaign bulletin Harbour Walk campaigners favour a weekend car-free trial. An online poll promoted through the campaign network received 150 responses, of which 120 supported the idea. Respondents were not asked where they lived. The campaign headline says that the town has spoken. Organisers acknowledge that people without internet access may not have seen the poll.\n\nSource B Access panel The access panel has not endorsed the plan. It wants a safe drop-off point, a continuous accessible route and a named contact for problems. It welcomes the aim of a more pleasant walking environment but says that support for an aim is not approval of a particular design. The campaign bulletin quoted its welcome without including this qualification.\n\nSource C Council note Visits to the area rose from 400 to 480 during the first demonstration weekend. These are recorded visits, not unique visitors. The weather was unusually fine and a music event took place nearby. The council has funds for signs for an eight-week trial, but no permanent scheme has been approved. A decision is due on Monday. Any announcement must describe the duration accurately and explain the access arrangements.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Calculate the poll support percentage and explain why it cannot establish town-wide support."},{"number":2,"question":"Give three access requirements and explain the panel's actual position."},{"number":3,"question":"Give the numerical and percentage increase in visits, and two reasons not to attribute it solely to the scheme."},{"number":4,"question":"Identify two ways a headline could overstate the council note."},{"number":5,"question":"Write a responsible two-sentence replacement for \"The whole town approves a permanent car-free zone.\""}]},"C":{"title":"C. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions before the first reading. Your coach reads the passage live twice. You may take notes; pause other work during the readings. Each question earns 4 marks: two details worth 2 marks each.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"State the change in weekly visits and the percentage increase."},{"number":2,"question":"How many registered members were there, and what extra provision complicates the comparison?"},{"number":3,"question":"Where should the correction appear, and why?"},{"number":4,"question":"What does \"a real adventure\" convey here, and what quotation permission exists?"},{"number":5,"question":"Who prepares the revised draft, and by when?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Writing","marks":30,"instruction":"Write a 260-300 word balanced commentary for a local news website about Harbour Walk. Use Sources A-C. Evaluate the campaign headline, explain what the figures can and cannot show, include the access panel fairly, and give a qualified recommendation. Add a separate 30-40 word note explaining one rhetorical or tone choice.","wordMin":260,"wordMax":300,"commentaryMin":30,"commentaryMax":40,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task and evidence","marks":10},{"criterion":"Organisation","marks":5},{"criterion":"Language range","marks":5},{"criterion":"Accuracy","marks":5},{"criterion":"Audience and commentary","marks":5}]}}},"11":{"version":4,"level":11,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 11","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Level_11_Candidate_Paper.pdf","instructions":"Answer every question. Work independently without a dictionary, phone or outside assistance. Use your own words for reading answers where possible; short, accurate answers are acceptable. All situations and data are fictional assessment material. Your coach reads the listening text live twice; pause other work during the readings. Speaking is assessed separately and does not change the core total out of 100. The website time limit is 45 minutes.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Language in context and production","marks":30,"instruction":"Questions 1\u201310: choose one answer. Questions 11\u201315: write one sentence for each task. Each response earns up to 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Were funding to be withdrawn, we ___ the timetable.","options":["would revise","had revised","revise","are revising"]},{"number":2,"question":"The proposal is feasible only ___ security is confirmed.","options":["whereas","provided that","despite","even"]},{"number":3,"question":"The figures lend ___ to the case for a trial.","options":["support","supporting","supported","supports"]},{"number":4,"question":"What remains uncertain ___ whether staffing can be sustained.","options":["are","were","is","have"]},{"number":5,"question":"The team denied ___ the consultation findings.","options":["to ignore","having ignored","ignore","has ignored"]},{"number":6,"question":"Which statement distinguishes correlation from causation?","options":["Visits rose, so the hours caused it.","The changes coincided, but other factors may explain the increase.","A rise proves the policy worked.","There can be only one explanation."]},{"number":7,"question":"The board recommended that the estimate ___ reviewed.","options":["is","was","be","being"]},{"number":8,"question":"No sooner had the meeting begun ___ an objection was raised.","options":["when","then","than","that"]},{"number":9,"question":"The decision is contingent ___ transport approval.","options":["on","of","at","by"]},{"number":10,"question":"Which response constructively mediates disagreement?","options":["Your concern is pointless.","We must choose without discussion.","Can we agree on the access criteria before comparing options?","The majority never needs evidence."]},{"number":11,"question":"Rewrite with were: If the grant were reduced, we would shorten the pilot."},{"number":12,"question":"Use a cleft beginning What: We need a confirmed security budget."},{"number":13,"question":"Politely challenge this inference: Most feedback was positive, so all users support expansion."},{"number":14,"question":"Summarise an agreement that depends on transport approval."},{"number":15,"question":"Correct: The committee suggested to extend the pilot."}]},"B":{"title":"B. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read all three sources, then answer using evidence from the sources. Each answer earns up to 4 marks.","passage":"Source A Trial report Eastbank recorded 180 visits during a trial of longer opening hours. Sixty feedback forms were received, of which 48 were positive. The report does not identify repeat visitors or whether one person submitted more than one form. A class moved into the building during the trial and publicity increased. The report recommends investigating demand rather than treating the visit count as proof of the effect of longer hours.\n\nSource B Youth group The youth group wants a full year of evening access. It can provide an activity leader but cannot supply security staff. Twenty-two of thirty people consulted during a daytime session said they preferred evenings. The group recognises that those absent from that session may have different needs. It asks the board to involve other users before finalising a shared timetable.\n\nSource C Finance note Funding currently covers eight weeks. An additional security estimate of 900 units has been received but not approved. No year-long funding is committed. The board can approve a limited pilot subject to staffing and funding, or defer it while seeking further evidence. A decision must name an accountable coordinator, set review criteria, and explain how shared access will be protected.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Calculate the positive-feedback percentage and give three limitations on generalising it to all users."},{"number":2,"question":"Give two factors other than added hours that may have affected visits and explain each."},{"number":3,"question":"Compare the youth group's staffing offer with the finance constraint."},{"number":4,"question":"Explain two limitations of the preference consultation."},{"number":5,"question":"Give four safeguards for a responsible pilot decision."}]},"C":{"title":"C. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions before the first reading. Your coach reads the passage live twice. You may take notes; pause other work during the readings. Each question earns 4 marks: two details worth 2 marks each.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"What was the delivery total, and why was the week unrepresentative?"},{"number":2,"question":"How long is the proposed extension, and who keeps the log?"},{"number":3,"question":"Which two approvals are needed?"},{"number":4,"question":"What two types of service data must be recorded?"},{"number":5,"question":"When will Noor contact transport, and what happens if approval is missing?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Writing","marks":30,"instruction":"Write a 300-350 word options brief for the Eastbank board. Compare a conditional eight-week pilot with deferral. Evaluate the evidence, distinguish stakeholder wishes from commitments, recommend one option, and specify implementation conditions and review criteria. Use Sources A-C. Add a separate 30-40 word note explaining one diplomatic language choice.","wordMin":300,"wordMax":350,"commentaryMin":30,"commentaryMax":40,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task and evidence","marks":10},{"criterion":"Organisation","marks":5},{"criterion":"Language range","marks":5},{"criterion":"Accuracy","marks":5},{"criterion":"Audience and commentary","marks":5}]}}},"12":{"version":4,"level":12,"title":"General English \u2014 Level 12","durationMinutes":45,"totalMarks":100,"sourceFile":"Level_12_Candidate_Paper.pdf","instructions":"Answer every question. Work independently without a dictionary, phone or outside assistance. Use your own words for reading answers where possible; short, accurate answers are acceptable. All situations and data are fictional assessment material. Your coach reads the listening text live twice; pause other work during the readings. Speaking is assessed separately and does not change the core total out of 100. The website time limit is 45 minutes.","sections":{"A":{"title":"A. Language in context and production","marks":30,"instruction":"Questions 1\u201310: choose one answer. Questions 11\u201315: write one sentence for each task. Each response earns up to 2 marks.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Had the discrepancy been disclosed, the editor ___ publication.","options":["may delay","might have delayed","delays","will delay"]},{"number":2,"question":"The evidence is compatible with the claim, ___ it does not establish it.","options":["yet","because","so that","unless"]},{"number":3,"question":"Which wording preserves uncertainty accurately?","options":["The witnesses agree completely.","The date is unquestionably correct.","The accounts differ on the date.","The newsletter must be false."]},{"number":4,"question":"Under no circumstances ___ an unverified quotation as confirmed.","options":["we should present","should we present","we present should","present we should"]},{"number":5,"question":"The archive contains accounts, the reliability of ___ varies.","options":["who","what","which","that"]},{"number":6,"question":"The report stops short of ___ a permanent service.","options":["recommend","to recommend","recommending","recommended"]},{"number":7,"question":"Which describes a source limitation rather than an attack on its author?","options":["The writer is foolish.","The account relies on one uncorroborated recollection.","The author should be ignored forever.","Everyone knows the writer is wrong."]},{"number":8,"question":"The apparent consensus may be an artefact ___ the sampling method.","options":["of","to","with","by"]},{"number":9,"question":"It is essential that anonymity ___ preserved.","options":["is being","be","was","to be"]},{"number":10,"question":"Which distinction matters most when reporting submissions?","options":["Long submissions are always true.","Every submission is one resident.","Duplicate entries and group submissions affect the unit counted.","More entries eliminate bias."]},{"number":11,"question":"Rewrite with inversion: We should never imply that silence means consent."},{"number":12,"question":"Qualify this causal claim without dismissing the evidence: The archive proves the closure caused migration."},{"number":13,"question":"Synthesize two conflicting accounts without choosing a date: Witness A says May; witness B says June."},{"number":14,"question":"Draft a sentence distinguishing current funding from a future commitment."},{"number":15,"question":"Correct: The evidence, together with the interviews, demonstrate consensus."}]},"B":{"title":"B. Reading","marks":20,"instruction":"Read all three sources, then answer using evidence from the sources. Each answer earns up to 4 marks.","passage":"Source A Archive inventory River Memory holds eighty recordings. Forty have permission for public use; twenty have permission only if contributors remain anonymous; permission for the remaining twenty is unresolved. These categories do not overlap. Thirty recordings discuss the same factory closure. The collection was assembled through personal networks, not a representative sampling process. Frequency within the archive therefore need not reflect prevalence across the town.\n\nSource B Editorial note Two witnesses give different dates for a meeting. A newsletter supplies a third date but may refer to a different meeting. The editor proposes presenting the disagreement transparently and checking records before assigning one definitive date. Removing every contradiction would make the story smoother but could hide an important limit of the evidence. A searchable transcript can support access, but publication must respect each contributor's permission.\n\nSource C Project budget Web funding covers three months. Support beyond that period has not been committed. Searchable text is included in the budget; continued audio hosting has not been costed. Six people tested the prototype on desktop computers. No tests used phones or assistive technology. The board must decide whether to approve a limited text-first release, delay for further testing, or seek a revised budget. A release plan needs a review date and a clear policy for unresolved permissions.","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Classify the eighty recordings by publication permission and explain why they cannot all be published immediately."},{"number":2,"question":"Explain why thirty accounts of the closure do not establish its importance to every resident."},{"number":3,"question":"Explain the date disagreement and propose an editorial response."},{"number":4,"question":"Identify four distinct limits in the budget and testing evidence."},{"number":5,"question":"Recommend a publication safeguard and justify it using three details across at least two sources."}]},"C":{"title":"C. Listening","marks":20,"instruction":"Read the questions before the first reading. Your coach reads the passage live twice. You may take notes; pause other work during the readings. Each question earns 4 marks: two details worth 2 marks each.","delivery":"coach-live","questions":[{"number":1,"question":"Give the original and deduplicated submission totals."},{"number":2,"question":"Why is the deduplicated total not a resident count, and what must be documented for audit?"},{"number":3,"question":"Among supporters of later opening, how many named Friday and how many named no day?"},{"number":4,"question":"What transport detail is unresolved, and what will the board do if it remains unresolved?"},{"number":5,"question":"Who corrects the counts and who revises the wording?"}]},"D":{"title":"D. Writing","marks":30,"instruction":"Write a 350-400 word publication proposal for the River Memory board. Synthesize Sources A-C, evaluate consent, source reliability, accessibility and sustainability, and recommend a defensible release strategy. Include a contingency and review criteria. Avoid presenting inference as established fact. Add a separate 50-60 word commentary explaining two editorial language choices.","wordMin":350,"wordMax":400,"commentaryMin":50,"commentaryMax":60,"rubric":[{"criterion":"Task and evidence","marks":10},{"criterion":"Organisation","marks":5},{"criterion":"Language range","marks":5},{"criterion":"Accuracy","marks":5},{"criterion":"Audience and commentary","marks":5}]}}}};

// Manual assignments/tests from Ask your coach → Submissions.
const SB_STUDENT_SUB_HEADERS=['Request ID','Received at (UTC)','Name','Reply email','Type','Subject','Message','Private attachments JSON','Fingerprint','Email status','Error'];
const SB_STUDENT_SUB_TYPES={pdf:'application/pdf',doc:'application/msword',docx:'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document',ppt:'application/vnd.ms-powerpoint',pptx:'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation',xls:'application/vnd.ms-excel',xlsx:'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet',txt:'text/plain',jpg:'image/jpeg',jpeg:'image/jpeg',png:'image/png',webp:'image/webp',gif:'image/gif',mp3:'audio/mpeg',wav:'audio/wav',m4a:'audio/mp4',ogg:'audio/ogg',mp4:'video/mp4',webm:'video/webm'};
function setupStudentSubmissions(){
 const book=sbExamBook(),props=PropertiesService.getScriptProperties();let sheet=book.getSheetByName('Student submissions');if(!sheet)sheet=book.insertSheet('Student submissions');if(!sheet.getLastRow()){sheet.appendRow(SB_STUDENT_SUB_HEADERS);sheet.setFrozenRows(1);}else if(JSON.stringify(sheet.getRange(1,1,1,11).getValues()[0])!==JSON.stringify(SB_STUDENT_SUB_HEADERS))throw Error('Submission headers differ. Existing data has not been replaced.');
 let id=props.getProperty('SB_STUDENT_SUBMISSIONS_FOLDER');if(id){if(DriveApp.getFolderById(id).isTrashed())throw Error('Restore the private submissions folder before continuing.');}else{const folder=DriveApp.createFolder('Speak Boldly — Private student submissions');folder.setSharing(DriveApp.Access.PRIVATE,DriveApp.Permission.NONE);id=folder.getId();props.setProperty('SB_STUDENT_SUBMISSIONS_FOLDER',id);}
 MailApp.getRemainingDailyQuota();props.setProperty('SB_STUDENT_SUBMISSIONS_ENABLED','true');console.log('Manual assignment/test submissions ready. New private folder and Student submissions sheet only; no client/session rows or signatures changed. Deploy a New version of the existing Clients web app.');
}
function sbStudentSubmission(data,requestSize){
 const json=x=>ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify(x)).setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON),lock=LockService.getScriptLock();
 try{
  const props=PropertiesService.getScriptProperties();if(props.getProperty('SB_STUDENT_SUBMISSIONS_ENABLED')!=='true')throw Error('Your coach needs to run setupStudentSubmissions and deploy the updated exam script.');
  if(requestSize>21000000)throw Error('Keep attachments within 15 MB total.');
  const requestId=String(data.requestId||''),name=String(data.name||'').trim(),email=String(data.email||'').trim().toLowerCase(),kind=String(data.kind||''),message=String(data.message||'').trim();
  if(data.website||! /^[a-zA-Z0-9-]{16,100}$/.test(requestId)||!name||name.length>100||/[\r\n]/.test(name)||! /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)||email.length>254||!['assignment','test'].includes(kind)||message.length>3900)throw Error('Check your name, email and submission type.');
  const files=sbStudentSubmissionFiles(data.attachments);if(!message&&!files.length)throw Error('Attach your work or write your answer before sending.');
  const subject=(kind==='test'?'Test':'Assignment')+' submission — '+name;
  const fingerprint=sbExamHash(JSON.stringify([name,email,kind,message,files.map(f=>[f.name,f.mimeType,f.bytes.length,Utilities.base64Encode(Utilities.computeDigest(Utilities.DigestAlgorithm.SHA_256,f.bytes))]) ]));
  lock.waitLock(20000);const sheet=sbExamBook().getSheetByName('Student submissions');if(!sheet)throw Error('Run setupStudentSubmissions first.');
  const rows=sheet.getLastRow()>1?sheet.getRange(2,1,sheet.getLastRow()-1,11).getValues():[],existing=rows.find(r=>String(r[0])===requestId);
  if(existing){if(String(existing[8])!==fingerprint)throw Error('This submission changed. Start a new submission.');if(existing[9]==='sent')return json({ok:true,status:'sent'});throw Error('Your submission is saved, but email delivery is not confirmed. Ask your coach to check Student submissions and Sent Mail before sending it again.');}
  const today=Utilities.formatDate(new Date(),'Africa/Cairo','yyyy-MM-dd'),daily=rows.filter(r=>Utilities.formatDate(new Date(r[1]),'Africa/Cairo','yyyy-MM-dd')===today);
  if(daily.length>=50||daily.filter(r=>String(r[3]).replace(/^'/,'')===email).length>=3)throw Error('The daily submission limit has been reached. Please try again tomorrow.');
  if(MailApp.getRemainingDailyQuota()<1)throw Error('Email is temporarily unavailable. Keep your files and try again later.');
  const folderId=props.getProperty('SB_STUDENT_SUBMISSIONS_FOLDER');if(!folderId)throw Error('Run setupStudentSubmissions first.');const folder=DriveApp.getFolderById(folderId);if(folder.isTrashed())throw Error('Your coach needs to restore the private submissions folder.');
  const row=sheet.getLastRow()+1,safe=v=>/^[=+\-@\t\r]/.test(v)?"'"+v:v;
  sheet.getRange(row,1,1,11).setValues([[requestId,new Date().toISOString(),safe(name),safe(email),kind,safe(subject),safe(message),'[]',fingerprint,'saving','']]);
  const attachments=[],stored=[];
  try{
   for(const f of files){const blob=Utilities.newBlob(f.bytes,f.mimeType,f.name),saved=folder.createFile(blob);attachments.push(blob);stored.push({id:saved.getId(),name:f.name,mimeType:f.mimeType,size:f.bytes.length});sheet.getRange(row,8).setValue(JSON.stringify(stored));}
   sheet.getRange(row,10,1,2).setValues([['sending','']]);SpreadsheetApp.flush();
   MailApp.sendEmail({to:'submissions@speakboldly.space',replyTo:email,name:'Speak Boldly',subject,attachments,body:(kind==='test'?'Test':'Assignment')+' submission\n\nName: '+name+'\nReply email: '+email+'\nSubmission ID: '+requestId+'\nAttachments: '+files.length+'\n\n'+(message||'(Work attached)')+'\n\nEnglish Language Coaching\n© 2026 Founded by Mira Nasser Louis.'});
   sheet.getRange(row,10,1,2).setValues([['sent','']]);return json({ok:true,status:'sent'});
  }catch(error){const status=String(sheet.getRange(row,10).getValues()[0][0])==='sending'?'delivery_unknown':'saved_incomplete';sheet.getRange(row,10,1,2).setValues([[status,String(error.message||error).slice(0,1000)]]);throw Error(status==='delivery_unknown'?'Your submission is saved, but email delivery is not confirmed. Ask your coach to check Student submissions and Sent Mail before sending it again.':'Your submission record is saved, but some attachments could not be stored. Keep your files and ask your coach to check the submission record before trying again.');}
 }catch(error){return json({ok:false,error:String(error.message||error)});}finally{if(lock.hasLock())lock.releaseLock();}
}
function sbStudentSubmissionFiles(input){
 if(input===undefined)return[];if(!Array.isArray(input)||input.length>10)throw Error('Attach up to 10 files.');let total=0;
 return input.map(value=>{if(!value||typeof value!=='object')throw Error('Invalid attachment.');const name=String(value.name||''),ext=name.split('.').pop().toLowerCase(),mimeType=SB_STUDENT_SUB_TYPES[ext],encoded=String(value.base64||'');
  if(!name||name.length>120||/[\r\n\\/]/.test(name)||!mimeType||value.mimeType!==mimeType||!encoded||encoded.length>6666668||! /^[A-Za-z0-9+/]*={0,2}$/.test(encoded))throw Error('Use supported files up to 5 MB each.');
  const bytes=Utilities.base64Decode(encoded);if(!bytes.length||bytes.length>5000000)throw Error('Each attachment must be between 1 byte and 5 MB.');total+=bytes.length;if(total>15000000)throw Error('Keep all attachments within 15 MB total.');
  const first=bytes.slice(0,12).map(b=>b&255),starts=expected=>expected.every((n,i)=>first[i]===n);
  if((ext==='pdf'&&!starts([37,80,68,70,45]))||(['jpg','jpeg'].includes(ext)&&!starts([255,216,255]))||(ext==='png'&&!starts([137,80,78,71,13,10,26,10]))||(ext==='gif'&&!starts([71,73,70,56]))||(ext==='webp'&&(!starts([82,73,70,70])||String.fromCharCode(...first.slice(8,12))!=='WEBP')))throw Error('The file contents do not match its format: '+name);
  return{name,mimeType,bytes};});
}

// Run once to open the newly uploaded papers; existing passwords/attempts remain intact.
function setupGeneralEnglishExams9To12(){
 if(PropertiesService.getScriptProperties().getProperty('SB_GE_EXAMS_READY')!=='true')setupGeneralEnglishExams();
 const lock=LockService.getScriptLock();lock.waitLock(20000);try{const props=PropertiesService.getScriptProperties();if(typeof sbSetupExamReviewGuide==='function')sbSetupExamReviewGuide();if(props.getProperty('SB_GE_9_12_READY')==='true'){console.log('Levels 9–12 were already added. Use the instructor exam cards to change passwords or access.');return;}const state=sbHubState();state.entries=state.entries||{};for(let level=9;level<=12;level++){const key='exams:'+level;state.entries[key]={...(state.entries[key]||{}),enabled:true};}state.revision=Utilities.getUuid();props.setProperty('SB_HUB_ACCESS_V1',JSON.stringify(state));props.setProperty('SB_GE_9_12_READY','true');console.log('Levels 9–12 enabled. Existing passwords and attempts preserved; no emails sent. Deploy a New version of the existing web app.');}finally{lock.releaseLock();}
}

function sbGeReviewLines(a){
 let guide={};try{guide=JSON.parse(PropertiesService.getScriptProperties().getProperty('SB_GE_REVIEW_GUIDE_'+a.level)||'{}');}catch{}
 const exam=SB_GE_CONTENT[a.level],lines=['SUGGESTED CORRECTIONS — INSTRUCTOR REVIEW REQUIRED','These are draft suggestions, not a final score. Check model answers against the task; accept accurate alternatives. Writing checks are rule-based and are not a complete grammar, vocabulary or meaning assessment.',''];
 for(const [key,section]of Object.entries(exam.sections)){
